---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The Data Platform — Case Study"
  description="Owned customer and order data infrastructure across 200+ merchants on five e-commerce platforms; freshness ranges from sub-5-minute (webhooks) to ~6 hours (legacy) vs. days-late baseline."
>
  <article class="py-13">
    <nav class="mb-8">
      <a href="/case-studies" class="text-14 text-accent no-underline">← Back to case studies</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start mb-6">
      <h1 class="font-code text-32 font-semibold m-0 flex-1">The Data Platform</h1>
      <button onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(window.location.href); this.textContent='Copied!'; setTimeout(() => this.textContent='Copy link', 2000);" class="px-4 py-2 border border-rule bg-transparent cursor-pointer font-code text-12 text-accent whitespace-nowrap rounded-[3px] transition-all duration-200" onmouseenter="this.style.borderColor='var(--accent)'" onmouseleave="this.style.borderColor='var(--rule)'">Copy link</button>
    </div>

    <p class="text-16 text-ink-2 mb-8 max-w-[65ch]">I owned the rebuild of customer and order data infrastructure across 200+ merchants on five commerce platforms. Freshness: sub-5-minute for webhook platforms, ~1–6 hours for others (vs. days-late baseline).</p>

    <div class="flex gap-4 flex-wrap mb-8">
      <span class="metric-chip">sub-5-minute to 6-hour tiered</span>
      <span class="metric-chip">200+ merchants</span>
      <span class="metric-chip">3-month migration · rollback path designed</span>
    </div>

    <p style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; color: var(--ink-3); margin-bottom: 40px;">Node.js · MySQL · Debezium · Google Pub/Sub · Datastream · MongoDB · BigQuery · ClickHouse · Redis · GKE</p>

    <!-- My Role -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-b border-rule pb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading mb-6">My Role</h2>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">What I personally owned</h3>
        <div class="grid gap-3 max-w-[65ch]">
          <div><strong>Architecture and technical direction</strong> — designed ingestion, CDC, and multi-layer serving architecture</div>
          <div><strong>CDC strategy</strong> — chose Debezium path, landing-layer design for reversibility</div>
          <div><strong>Migration strategy</strong> — planned and executed store-by-store cutover across 200+ merchants</div>
          <div><strong>Production rollout</strong> — owned monitoring, alerting, and incident response across about five years of platform ownership</div>
          <div><strong>Core implementation</strong> — architected and directed the CDC pipeline, landing store, and warehouse consolidation; team (4→7 engineers) built and maintained</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">What the team delivered</h3>
        <div class="grid gap-3 max-w-[65ch]">
          <div><strong>Platform team grew from 4 to 7 engineers</strong> — directly managed the team (1:1s, code review, hiring decisions) alongside architecture and deployment strategy</div>
          <div><strong>Team delivery</strong> — continuous production monitoring, reliability work, and incremental optimization</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Context</h2>
      <p class="prose-body">The platform serves 200+ e-commerce merchants across five platforms (Shopify, BigCommerce, WooCommerce, Magento 1, Magento 2). Customer data, orders, click-tracking, and loyalty data all flow through separate systems with no unified schema. Downstream products (recommendations, segmentation, attribution) need to access this data, but latency and availability are unpredictable.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Problem</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mb-6">Data arrives days late. Commerce webhooks provide near-real-time events, but custom platform integrations require full data downloads every 6 hours. Click-tracking and loyalty data are even slower. Downstream products can't build real-time features. Reporting is expensive because queries have to reprocess data constantly. New products need to build custom integrations instead of tapping a shared stream.</p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-8 mt-6">
        <div class="p-5 bg-panel rounded">
          <div style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 12px;">BEFORE</div>
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">6+ hour batch delays</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">Custom integrations per product</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">Expensive BigQuery reporting</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink">No unified data model</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 bg-panel rounded">
          <div style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 12px;">WHY IT MATTERED</div>
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">Real-time recommendations impossible</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">Segmentation used stale data</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink mb-2">Attribution couldn't link orders to blocks</li>
            <li class="text-14 leading-[1.6] text-ink">Every new feature required engineering</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Architecture -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Architecture</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mb-6">Consolidates four source systems (commerce, click-tracking, loyalty, CRM) into a raw MySQL landing store, preserving original schema. Debezium streams changes via Google Pub/Sub. Downstream layer fans out to three specialized stores: MongoDB for point lookups (serving), ClickHouse for analytics, BigQuery for historical data.</p>

      <!-- Architecture SVG Diagram -->
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 480" style="max-width: 65ch; width: 100%; margin-bottom: 24px; display: block;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs>
          <style>
            .arch-box { fill: var(--panel); stroke: var(--rule-2); stroke-width: 1.5; }
            .arch-label { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; fill: var(--ink); }
            .arch-label-small { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 10px; fill: var(--ink-2); }
            .arch-arrow { stroke: var(--ink-3); stroke-width: 2; fill: none; marker-end: url(#arrowhead); }
            .arch-accent { fill: var(--accent); }
          </style>
          <marker id="arrowhead" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="9" refY="3" orient="auto">
            <polygon points="0 0, 10 3, 0 6" fill="var(--ink-3)" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Sources: Commerce, Click-tracking, Loyalty, CRM -->
        <rect class="arch-box" x="20" y="20" width="140" height="50" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="90" y="50" text-anchor="middle">Commerce</text>

        <rect class="arch-box" x="180" y="20" width="140" height="50" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="250" y="50" text-anchor="middle">Click-tracking</text>

        <rect class="arch-box" x="340" y="20" width="140" height="50" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="410" y="50" text-anchor="middle">Loyalty</text>

        <rect class="arch-box" x="500" y="20" width="120" height="50" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="560" y="50" text-anchor="middle">CRM</text>

        <!-- Arrows from sources -->
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="90" y1="70" x2="120" y2="110"/>
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="250" y1="70" x2="200" y2="110"/>
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="410" y1="70" x2="380" y2="110"/>
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="560" y1="70" x2="450" y2="110"/>

        <!-- MySQL Landing Layer -->
        <rect class="arch-box" x="60" y="110" width="440" height="60" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="290" y="138" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">MySQL Landing Layer</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="290" y="155" text-anchor="middle">(raw, per-source schemas, reversible landing layer)</text>

        <!-- Arrow to CDC -->
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="290" y1="170" x2="290" y2="210"/>

        <!-- Debezium CDC + Pub/Sub -->
        <rect class="arch-box" x="80" y="210" width="420" height="60" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="290" y="238" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Debezium CDC → Google Pub/Sub</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="290" y="255" text-anchor="middle">(replay boundary, per-tenant isolation)</text>

        <!-- Arrows to serving layer -->
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="160" y1="270" x2="100" y2="310"/>
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="290" y1="270" x2="290" y2="310"/>
        <line class="arch-arrow" x1="420" y1="270" x2="480" y2="310"/>

        <!-- Serving Layer: MongoDB, ClickHouse, BigQuery -->
        <rect class="arch-box" x="20" y="310" width="140" height="70" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="90" y="335" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">MongoDB</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="90" y="355" text-anchor="middle">(serving)</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="90" y="370" text-anchor="middle">point lookups</text>

        <rect class="arch-box" x="200" y="310" width="140" height="70" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="270" y="335" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">ClickHouse</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="270" y="355" text-anchor="middle">(analytics)</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="270" y="370" text-anchor="middle">aggregation</text>

        <rect class="arch-box" x="380" y="310" width="140" height="70" rx="4"/>
        <text class="arch-label" x="450" y="335" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">BigQuery</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="450" y="355" text-anchor="middle">(historical)</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="450" y="370" text-anchor="middle">audit trail</text>

        <!-- Ownership & SLO annotations -->
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="290" y="190" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: var(--accent); font-size: 9px;">Platform team owns</text>
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="290" y="280" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: var(--accent); font-size: 9px;">5min alert, 15min page</text>

        <!-- Consumption callout -->
        <text class="arch-label-small" x="290" y="430" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: var(--accent);">← Recommendations, Segmentation, Attribution, Reporting</text>
      </svg>

      <p class="text-14 text-ink-2 max-w-[65ch]">New sources plug into the landing layer. New consumers subscribe to Pub/Sub. No custom integrations required. Platform team owns the landing layer and CDC; 5-minute latency alerts, 15-minute on-call page threshold.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ClickHouse Tuning -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">ClickHouse Tuning</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mb-6">Migrated segmentation analytics from BigQuery to ClickHouse to reduce cost 77.8% (₹1.8L → ₹40K/month) and achieved sub-second response latency on million-row aggregations. Tuning choices:</p>
      <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 max-w-[65ch]">
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3"><strong>Partitioning:</strong> <code class="font-code text-accent">(client_id, date)</code> to isolate per-store backfills and date-range scans.</li>
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3"><strong>Order by:</strong> <code class="font-code text-accent">date</code> for both segmentation filters and time-series reporting queries.</li>
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">Materialized views: Hourly batch refresh instead of real-time (stable CPU, predictable cost).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Key Decisions -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Key Decisions</h2>
      <ol class="max-w-[65ch] list-none p-0">
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-5">
          <strong>Landed raw instead of normalizing at ingest</strong><br/>
          <span class="text-ink-2">Costs: five schemas instead of one. Benefit: reversibility. Wrong normalization at ingest means multi-service migration. Wrong view is a redefinition away from correction.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-5">
          <strong>CDC from landing store, not sources</strong><br/>
          <span class="text-ink-2">Costs: one more moving part. Benefit: CDC from a consolidated landing layer with replay and validation boundaries.</span>
        </li>
        <li style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 20px;">
          <strong>Two replication paths (Datastream + Debezium)</strong><br/>
          <span class="text-ink-2">Started with managed Datastream, gradually earned our way to pure Debezium. Avoided speculative engineering while managing risk.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">
          <strong>Table-per-client multiplies operational surface</strong><br/>
          <span class="text-ink-2">Thousands of tables, migrations run across all. Accepted because shared-table contention is worse and less visible than operational overhead.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Execution -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Execution</h2>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">Migration Strategy</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch]">Store-by-store cutover because the data layer sits under every product. Bad cutover breaks all of them simultaneously. Slow but safe.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">Backfill & Validation</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch]">Dual-wrote to old and new landing layers for 2 weeks. Hourly validation compared row counts, key distributions, timestamp ranges, and checksums across migrated tables before each cutover.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">Rollback Strategy</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch]">Kept old pipeline running for 30 days after cutover. If anything broke, could revert to old data. Never had to use it, but reduced risk psychologically and operationally.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">Monitoring & Alerting</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch]">Added metrics for data freshness (time from source to landing to serving), lag per store, checkpoint latency. Alerts on 5-minute latency deviation. Page on-call for >15 min delays.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin: 32px 0; padding: 24px; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px;">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">The production incident that changed the design</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch] mb-3">Debezium's snapshot query on 50M rows consumed 60% CPU and blocked production writes. We stopped rollout immediately and used Datastream to seed the baseline instead, then transitioned tables to Debezium incrementally.</p>
        <p class="prose-body"><a href="/engineering-notes/debezium-50m-row-snapshot/" class="plain-link">Read the full incident and recovery decisions →</a></p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin: 32px 0; padding: 24px; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px;">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">The consumer crashes that weren't a memory leak</h3>
        <p class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink max-w-[65ch] mb-3">Pub/Sub consumers crashing at scale. Root cause: Kubernetes pod memory limits, not application code. Consumers pulled messages as fast as possible with no backpressure. Fixed by monitoring heap usage and pausing consumption at a high-water mark, resuming when safe.</p>
        <p class="prose-body"><a href="/case-studies/hard-problems#incident-10" class="plain-link">Read the full incident analysis →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Impact -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Impact</h2>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 32px; padding: 32px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: center;">Data Freshness by Platform Capability</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto max-w-[65ch]">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-14 leading-[1.6]">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b-2 border-rule">
                <th class="text-left px-2 py-3 font-semibold text-accent">Client platform capability</th>
                <th class="text-left px-2 py-3 font-semibold text-accent">Data freshness</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-rule">
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink">Native webhook support (e.g. Shopify, BigCommerce)</td>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink font-code font-medium">&lt; 5 minutes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-rule">
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink">REST API support, no webhooks</td>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink font-code font-medium">~1 hour</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-rule">
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink">Incremental sync only</td>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink font-code font-medium">~2 hours</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-rule">
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink">Full data resync required every time</td>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink font-code font-medium">6 hours</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink">Legacy cron-based system (before rearchitecture, large clients)</td>
                <td class="px-2 py-3 text-ink font-code font-medium">up to several days</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">Why It Mattered</h3>
        <ul class="max-w-[65ch] list-none p-0">
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">Real-time recommendations became possible. Merchants could see recommendations update within seconds of customer actions.</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">Conversion attribution could link orders to specific blocks and email clicks — what proved to merchants that recommendations were actually working.</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">Segmentation worked with fresh data. Audiences updated in minutes, not hours.</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">Reporting moved off a cost curve that scaled with query volume onto one the team controlled.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold text-accent mb-3">Scale</h3>
        <ul class="max-w-[65ch] list-none p-0">
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">200+ merchants · 5 platforms · 3-month migration · rollback path remained available and was not used</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">New sources (Klaviyo, Zendesk) plugged in as streaming integrations, not custom pipelines</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">Completed migration off legacy architecture within 3 months with the rollback path available throughout cutover</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What I'd Change Today -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="section-heading">What I'd Change Today</h2>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">What I'd Preserve</h3>
        <ul class="max-w-[65ch] list-none p-0">
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink mb-3">Raw landing layer (reversibility principle held)</li>
          <li style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 12px;">CDC at the landing store, not each source, to keep downstream replay and validation centralized</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">Table-per-tenant for isolation (multi-tenant contention is worse than ops cost)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-14 font-semibold mb-3">What I'd Change</h3>
        <ul style="max-width: 65ch; list-style: none; padding: 0;">
          <li style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Datastream → pure Debezium from day one.</strong> Datastream was our transition strategy; we could have earned off it faster. Debezium proved itself in production.</li>
          <li style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Consider separate CDC per domain.</strong> Commerce CDC vs. click-tracking CDC would reduce blast radius of unplanned events.</li>
          <li class="text-16 leading-[1.8] text-ink">​<strong>Evaluate Iceberg for analytics layer.</strong> Wasn't production-ready at the time; would simplify the architecture today.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section style="padding: 52px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 52px;">
      <p class="font-code text-12 text-accent font-semibold mb-4">Next steps</p>
      <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0">
        <li class="mb-3">→ <a href="/case-studies/hard-problems" class="plain-link">See production incidents that shaped this design</a></li>
        <li class="mb-3">→ <a href="/resume" class="plain-link">Download my resume</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .case-next { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; padding-top: 32px; margin-top: 48px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
</style>
